SUMMARY:What Enterprises Look for in OSS Under the EU Cyber Resilience Act
DESCRIPTION:All Things Open RTP Meetup\nWe're back to in-person meetups fo
 llowing the uber-successful All Things AI event we co-hosted March 23 & 24
 !\n\nThe topic and speaker are super interesting to us and should deliver 
 a ton of value and education to everyone in attendance.\n\nThe topic - **W
 hat Enterprises Look for in OSS Under the EU Cyber Resilience Act — and 
 How Maintainers Can Deliver It**\, is important because many companies in 
 the RTP and surrounding areas\, and in our community\, offer software prod
 ucts that no longer stay within the U.S. market\, especially in the open s
 ource ecosystem. And because the EU Cyber Resilience Act makes cybersecuri
 ty a legal requirement\, the penalty for non-compliance can be severe.\n\n
 The speaker\, Dr. Lisa Bradley\, is a distinguished cybersecurity expert a
 nd visionary leader\, currently serving as the Senior Director of Product 
 & Application Security at Dell Technologies.\n\n**Start Time:**\n\n* Doors
  open at 6:00 pm for networking\, food and beverages\, and (free) swag\n* 
 Live programming begins at 6:30 pm sharp\n\n**Location:**\nWake Technical 
 Community College - RTP Campus\n10908 Chapel Hill Rd.\nMorrisville\, NC 27
 560\n*\\*We'll be in the auditorium\, which is located in building RT1\, r
 oom 162. Enter through the side entrance - we'll be there to guide you alo
 ng.*\n\n**Cost:** FREE\, as always\n\n**Topic:**\nWhat Enterprises Look fo
 r in OSS Under the EU Cyber Resilience Act — and How Maintainers Can Del
 iver It\n*Subtitle: Turning EU Cyber Resilience Act requirements into acti
 onable guidance for open-source maintainers.*\n\n**Abstract:**\nThe **EU C
 yber Resilience Act (CRA)** introduces mandatory cybersecurity requirement
 s for hardware and software “products with digital elements” sold in t
 he EU\, including lifecycle‑long obligations for secure design\, vulnera
 bility handling\, and timely security updates. While non‑commercial open
 ‑source software (OSS) is exempt in some scenarios\, OSS that is integra
 ted into commercial products—or forms part of a connected system—can s
 till impact a company’s compliance obligations. As a result\, OSS projec
 ts increasingly influence whether downstream manufacturers can meet CRA re
 quirements.\n\nThis session explains what big enterprises now look for whe
 n selecting OSS under the CRA\, including clear vulnerability reporting pr
 actices\, transparent support expectations\, and evidence of secure develo
 pment. It outlines how companies are building **trustworthiness scoring mo
 dels** to evaluate OSS components\, identify projects that fall outside ac
 ceptable risk thresholds\, and decide when to invest\, mitigate\, or repla
 ce them. Most importantly\, maintainers will learn the practical steps the
 y can take—without becoming full‑time compliance teams—to make their
  projects the *trusted\, low‑friction choice* for corporations operating
  under the CRA.\n\n**Questions?** Contact us at [info@allthingsopen.org.](
 http://info@allthingsopen.org./)\n\n**RSVP required**\n\\*This is a free e
 vent. However\, an RSVP is required. Seating is limited\, so RSVP's will b
 e capped as we fill up. Please move quickly and RSVP to save your spot if 
 interested. When you RSVP you will be added to our mailing list to receive
  future event updates. You can unsubscribe at any time.
